# Build an Agent Workflow

A Workflow is a directed acyclic graph of Agent invocations, authored as a
`kind: Workflow` YAML file. Each step names an Agent, by a relative path to a
local Agent file or by the `CardRef` of a registered Agent. A step runs once
the steps in its `depends_on` have finished; steps with no dependency between
them run at the same time.

Dependencies only order steps. Data moves through explicit declarations:

- `spec.inputs` declares the Workflow inputs and their defaults.
- A step's `inputs` binds each unresolved Prompt variable of its Agent to `input.<name>`, a dependency's `steps.<id>.output.text`, or `steps.<id>.output.structured[.<field>...]`.
- `spec.outputs` names the values the run returns.

A step whose Prompt variable has no binding, or a binding that names a step the step does not depend on, fails validation before any model call.

## Author

This Workflow, from `examples/workflows/code-review`, runs a security review
and a correctness review in parallel, then hands both to a final reviewer:

<CodeFromFile file="examples/workflows/code-review/workflow.yaml" lang="yaml" title="workflow.yaml" />

Load it with `Workflow.from_path`, or parse YAML text with `Workflow.from_yaml`:

```python
from wyrd import Workflow

wf = Workflow.from_path("examples/workflows/code-review/workflow.yaml")
print(wf.steps)  # ["security", "correctness", "final_review"]
```

`Workflow.from_path` loads local Agent and Prompt files next to the Workflow
without a server. Steps that reference registered Agents are read from the
Wyrd server as the `client` you pass, or through your configured credentials
when you pass none. `Workflow.from_yaml` takes the text of a Workflow whose
Agents are inline.

The Rust Skald runtime also builds Workflows in code with
`Workflow::sequential`, `Workflow::parallel`, `add`, and `add_after`:

<CodeFromFile file="examples/rust/workflow_from_builder.rs" lang="rust" />

## Run

```python
run = wf.run({"code": "diff --git a/src/auth.rs b/src/auth.rs\n..."})
print(run.status)    # "succeeded", "failed", "cancelled", or "timed_out"
print(run.outputs)   # the named outputs declared under spec.outputs
print(run.steps["security"]["text"])
```

Mapping input supplies declared inputs by name; undeclared keys and values of the wrong declared type are rejected. A string is shorthand for the declared string input named `input`. Validation, input, and route errors raise `WyrdError` before any step runs. Once the run starts, step failures are recorded in `run.steps` and `run.error` instead of being raised.

## Run From The CLI

`wyrd workflow run` takes exactly one source: `--file <path>`, `--uid <uid>`,
or `--space <space> --name <name> --version <version>`. Input is a JSON object
from `--input '<json>'` or `--input-file <path>`. Runs execute in the CLI
process by default; `--execution server` runs a registered Workflow on the
server. `wyrd workflow` commands take no `--server` option; the Wyrd endpoint
and credential come from the same client configuration the SDKs read, such as
`WYRD_SERVER_URL`.

```bash
wyrd workflow run --file examples/workflows/code-review/workflow.yaml \
  --input-file examples/workflows/code-review/input.json
wyrd apply examples/workflows/code-review
wyrd workflow run --space engineering --name code-review --version 1.0.0 \
  --input-file examples/workflows/code-review/input.json --execution server
```

`--format json` prints the `WorkflowRun` snapshot; text output prints the
status and named outputs, and `--steps` adds every step's result. The command
exits `0` when the run succeeded and `2` when it ended in any other status.

A server run prints `accepted workflow run <id>` to stderr, then waits for the
run to finish. `--detach` returns as soon as the run is accepted. Interrupting
the wait exits `130` and leaves the run running on the server; it is never
cancelled or resubmitted. Use the ID afterwards:

```bash
wyrd workflow status <run-id> --steps
wyrd workflow cancel <run-id>
```

Server runs live in the server process that accepted them. A restart loses
them, and a multi-replica deployment must route one run's requests to the
same replica.

## Route Model Calls

`llm_route` on the Workflow, or on one step to override it, selects where each
Agent's model calls go:

- `native` (default) calls the provider from the CLI or SDK process. Server
  runs refuse it.
- `wyrd_gateway` calls the governed Wyrd gateway with your identity. A step
  `fallback` travels with each call. Local runs reach the public Chat
  Completions, Responses, Anthropic Messages, and Gemini endpoints; a Vertex
  Prompt runs only on the server.
- `ext_gateway` sends the native request straight to an external gateway
  binding. Local runs read the binding from the client `config.toml`:

```toml
[workflow.external_gateway_bindings.review-gateway]
protocol = "openai_chat"
origin = "https://gateway.example.com"
secret_headers = { x-gateway-key = { source = "file", path = "/run/secrets/gateway-key" } }
```

The secret is read when a run that selects the binding starts, never while
loading or registering, and never appears in run output. Server runs use the
bindings the server operator assigns to your tenant.

